1. INTRODUCTION: THE QUADRUPLE CHALLENGE
Modern safety-critical systems face four distinct certification regimes:
Domain Standard Primary Concern
Automotive ISO 26262 Functional safety in road vehicles
Industrial IEC 61508 Functional safety in E/E/PE systems
Aerospace DO-178C / ED-12C Software safety in airborne systems
Cybersecurity IEC 62443 Security in industrial automation
Historically, these have been treated as separate silos. A system designed for automotive
safety does not automatically satisfy aerospace requirements. A secure system is not
necessarily safe.
ST-T1024 challenges this separation.
The thesis of this paper is simple: A system that is deterministic, auditable, and physically
bounded satisfies the foundational requirements of all four norms simultaneously.

This paper demonstrates that ST-T1024—an open standard for deterministic,
ultra-low-energy, sovereign AI inference—provides a hardware-enforced foundation that
aligns with all four standards. It does not claim certification has been achieved. It claims the
architectural properties required for certification exist, and the path to certification is clear.
